          "extracted_fact": "And for Meta, we expect production shipments of their custom MTIA accelerator optimized for inference and recommendation at scale.\" [...] \"During the quarter, we delivered Ironwood TPU v7, version 7 in high volume to both Anthropic and Google. Broadcom continued to ride demand for its AI custom accelerators in the fiscal third quarter with revenue growth of 86%.",
          "excerpt": "\"In Q3, we also shipped Jalapeno, OpenAI's first-generation custom accelerator, which outperforms Grace Blackwell GPU for inference workloads. Overall, our XPU shipments for the third quarter were up over 3.5x year-on-year and represented 73% of AI revenue during the quarter. Our AI networking revenue was up over 2.5x year-on-year. And this strong momentum continues into Q4.\"\n \"During the quarter, we expect to accelerate shipments of Ironwood to Anthropic. We also expect to ramp up high-volume shipments of the TPU version 8i to Google. Shipments of Jalapeno for OpenAI will continue. And for Meta, we expect production shipments of their custom MTIA accelerator optimized for inference and recommendation at scale.\" [...] \"During the quarter, we delivered Ironwood TPU v7, version 7 in high volume to both Anthropic and Google. At the same time, we began production shipments of the next-generation TPU version 8i for Google. This new TPU generation has been designed with more memory and bandwidth compared to Ironwood, and just like Ironwood, is optimized for inference workloads. And in performance, it is comparable, if not surpasses the Vera Rubin GPU. Just to reiterate the major technology challenges of developing these complex accelerators, Broadcom is now shipping the TPU version 8i ahead of the MediaTek version v8t, which, in fact, was initiated earlier.\" [...] Customer Stories\n\nThese enterprises are making big waves with technology use cases.\n\n# Broadcom reports strong Q3, sees",
          "page_text": "\"In Q3, we also shipped Jalapeno, OpenAI's first-generation custom accelerator, which outperforms Grace Blackwell GPU for inference workloads. Overall, our XPU shipments for the third quarter were up over 3.5x year-on-year and represented 73% of AI revenue during the quarter. Our AI networking revenue was up over 2.5x year-on-year. And this strong momentum continues into Q4.\"\n \"During the quarter, we expect to accelerate shipments of Ironwood to Anthropic. We also expect to ramp up high-volume shipments of the TPU version 8i to Google. Shipments of Jalapeno for OpenAI will continue. And for Meta, we expect production shipments of their custom MTIA accelerator optimized for inference and recommendation at scale.\" [...] \"During the quarter, we delivered Ironwood TPU v7, version 7 in high volume to both Anthropic and Google. At the same time, we began production shipments of the next-generation TPU version 8i for Google. This new TPU generation has been designed with more memory and bandwidth compared to Ironwood, and just like Ironwood, is optimized for inference workloads. And in performance, it is comparable, if not surpasses the Vera Rubin GPU. Just to reiterate the major technology challenges of developing these complex accelerators, Broadcom is now shipping the TPU version 8i ahead of the MediaTek version v8t, which, in fact, was initiated earlier.\" [...] Customer Stories\n\nThese enterprises are making big waves with technology use cases.\n\n# Broadcom reports strong Q3, sees AI chip revenue accelerating in Q4\n\nPublished September 2, 2026\n\nCopy link Link Copied\n\nCopied to clipboard\n\nBroadcom continued to ride demand for its AI custom accelerators in the fiscal third quarter with revenue growth of 86%.\n\nThe company reported third quarter earnings of $2.68 a share on revenue of $29.6 billion, up 86% from a year ago. Non-GAAP earnings were $3.32 a share.\n\nWall Street was expecting Broadcom to report non-GAAP earnings of $3.24 a share on revenue of $29.43 billion.\n\nBroadcom projected fourth quarter revenue of $34.8 billion, up 93% from a year ago.\n[![Constellation Research Site Logo](/themes/custom/constellationr/assets/img/site-header-logo.svg)](/)\n\n[![Constellation Research Site Logo](/themes/custom/constellationr/assets/img/icons/small-logo-circles.svg)](/)\n\n[![Search Icon](/themes/custom/constellationr/assets/img/icons/orange-search-sm.svg)](/search)\n\n#### Research\n\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/book-open-01.svg)\n\n  [Full Library](/research/library)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/edit-white.svg)\n\n  [Analyst Blog](/research/blog)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/users-01.svg)\n\n  [Analysts](/research/analysts)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2026/03/book-open-01.svg)\n\n  [The Board](/the-board)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2026/01/file-search-02.svg)\n\n  [ShortLists](/research/shortlist-overview)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/folder.svg)\n\n  [Business Themes](/research/themes)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/layout-alt-02.svg)\n\n  [Research Agenda](/research/agenda)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2026/05/book-open-01.svg)\n\n  [Orbits Series](/research/library/orbits-series)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/shield-tick.svg)\n\n  [Research & Citation Policies](/research/policies)\n\n#### Recommendations for You\n\n[Analyst Blog](/research/blog)\n\nGet updates about industry trends, events and research.\n\n[Meet Our Analysts](/research/contributors)\n\nConnect with our subject matter experts.\n\n#### Videos\n\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/play-square.svg)\n\n  [DisrupTV](/video/channel/disruptv)\n* ![](/sites/default/files/mega-menu-link-icon/2025/11/play-square_1.svg)\n\n  [ConstellationTV](/video/channel/constellationtv)\n* ![](/sites/default